#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# gamma_regularized

#% text
# `gamma_regularized(a, x)` gives the regularized upper incomplete gamma function `gamma(a, x)/gamma(a)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `gamma_regularized(a, x)` is `Q(a, x)` of DLMF 8.2.4: the integral of `t^(a - 1)*exp(-t)` from `x` to `inf`, divided by `gamma(a)`. It falls from 1 at `x = 0` to 0.

#% item
# `1 - gamma_regularized(a, x)` is the lower function `P(a, x)`, the distribution function of the gamma distribution.

#% item
# Integer `a` with exact `x` gives exact values; machine numbers and balls give numerical values.

#% item
# `gamma_regularized` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
gamma_regularized(2, 1)
#% output
#= 2/e

#% cell
gamma_regularized(2.5, 1.0)
#% output
#= 0.8491450360846097

#% cell
gamma_regularized(1, x)
#% output
#= exp(-x)

#% cell
n(gamma_regularized(1/2, 2), 30)
#% output
#= 0.0455002638963584144005652743330669

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Large parameters:

#% cell
gamma_regularized(100.0, 90.0)
#% output
#= 0.8417790108135699

#% text
# Several points:

#% cell
gamma_regularized(3.0, [0.5, 1.0, 2.0])
#% output
#= [0.9856123220330293, 0.9196986029286058, 0.6766764161830635]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The probability that a chi-square value with 4 degrees of freedom exceeds 9.49:

#% cell
gamma_regularized(2.0, 9.49/2)
#% output
#= 0.049953131223294894

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The inverse in the second argument:

#% cell
inverse_gamma_regularized(2.5, gamma_regularized(2.5, 1.3))
#% output
#= 1.3

